view-if: year = 1951 and month = 1

= The Crisis over West Irian

In parliament, there was widespread agreement that the Netherlands-Indonesian Union, which had acquired little reality in the year it had been in existence, should be liquidated. And some kind of revision of the Round Table Conference agreements was called for by almost all groups of parliamentarians. There were, however, differences of emphasis between the viewpoints of government-supporting and opposition parties.

These differences eventually came to the fore as a motion has been placed before the House. The motion of five government-supporting parties was moved by Djody Gondokusumo of the <span style="color: #545454;">**PRN**</span>. It urged the government:

> *(1) to be prepared to conduct further negotiations with the Netherlands on the basis of the transfer of Indonesian sovereignty over West Irian;*
> *(2) to give the Netherlands at most two months to answer the Indonesian demand as in (1) above; and*
> *(3) to abrogate the Union Statute and review the results of the Round Table Conference and other agreements with the Netherlands with a view to abrogating all agreements which are damaging to Indonesia if there is no Dutch answer, or no satisfactory Dutch answer, within the time stipulated above.*

The cabinet declared that it could not accept the Kusnan motion, but that there was little difference between its own standpoint and that incorporated in the Djody motion. It stated that it would negotiate with the Netherlands only on the basis of the transfer of sovereignty over Irian. And it said it would appoint a Special State Commission to examine the Round Table Conference agreements with a view to seeing how they could be brought into line with the new situation created by the failure of the Irian negotiations. Thereupon Djody withdrew his motion. 

- @kusnan: But wait, there's more..

@kusnan
on-arrival: psii_relation += 5;

Another slightly stronger motion put on the same time as Djody's was that of Rahendra Kusnan, signed by <span style="color: #990000;">**PNI**</span> and <span style="color: #003319;">**PSII**</span> members. It urged the government:

> *(1) to continue to demand the immediate incorporation of West Irian into the territory of the Republic of Indonesia, on the basis of it having the same status as other areas of Indonesia;*
> *(2) to abolish the Netherlands-Indonesian Union; and*
> *(3) to review the other results of the Round Table Conference within three months with a view to obtaining their abrogation.*

A vote was taken on the Kusnan PNI-PSII motion. 

- @kusnan_nay: The motion is defeated..

@kusnan_nay
subtitle: 66 to 63

The government parties were unable to muster anything like full cohesion, with government parties, like the <span style="color: #FF6666;">**PSI**</span>, abstaining from the vote. But the motion was somehow defeated, by the narrow margin of 66 to 63. 

Eventually, the immediate crisis has passed.

@hadikusumo: Or has it?

But this does not mean that the Natsir cabinet's position is secure. 

Just last month, a parliamentary motion was tabled asking the government to revoke Regulation 39 and "freeze" the councils established on the basis of it. The list of signatures on the motion was headed by our S. Hadikusumo and included representatives of two other opposition parties, the <span style="color: #ff0000;">Labor</span> Party and the <span style="color: #003319;">**PSII**</span>. More dangerously for the cabinet however, it also included representatives of three government parties, <span style="color: #ff9933;">**PIR**</span>, <span style="color: #b9a568;">**Parindra**</span>, and the <span style="color: #66B2FF;">**Parkindo**</span>. In the following month demands were made by party branches and multiparty committees in different parts of Java and Sumatra that the controversial regulation be revoked.

When the government came to present its case against the Hadikusumo motion in January, it declared itself in agreement with many criticisms of Regulation 39, but added that merely to dissolve the great number of councils which had already been established on the basis of it would be to produce chaos and confusion. Critics of the regulation should, the government maintained, support the early holding of elections for the regional assemblies.

Only twelve days after the Natsir cabinet narrowly survived the test of the Kusnan motion, the Hadikusumo motion is put.

- @hadikusumo_yay: The motion succeeds!

@hadikusumo_yay
subtitle: 70 to 48
on-arrival: rural_masyumi -= 10

The <span style="color: #ff9933;">**PIR**</span>, <span style="color: #b9a568;">**Parindra**</span>, and the <span style="color: #66B2FF;">**Parkindo**</span> voted with the opposition. Several other government parties, including the <span style="color: #FF6666;">**PSI**</span>, abstained. The result was a defeat for the government by the large margin of 70 to 48.

Natsir stated that a clear conflict has consisted between cabinet and parliament. He challenged parliament to choose between yielding on the matter of the regional councils or moving a direct motion of no confidence in the cabinet.

- @root: Natsir's tenure is nearing its end..